/// The `{{name}}` system. A system is any `fn(&mut Resources)`.
///
/// # It registers itself
///
/// You do not add it anywhere. The editor watches `src/` and rewrites
/// `src/registrations.rs` when it changes — so saving this file is
/// enough, from here or from any other editor.
///
/// ⚠️ Registering is not compiling. The generated file names your system
/// within a second; the editor still runs the *last build* of your
/// project until you rebuild it. The toolbar's Resync button pulses
/// while those two disagree.
///
/// # `#[system(...)]` — where it runs
///
/// Without the attribute a system lands in `Stage::Update`, gated so it
/// runs only while playing. The attribute says otherwise:
///
/// ```ignore
/// #[system]                     // Update, gated by Play — the default
/// #[system(PreUpdate)]          // a different stage, still gated
/// #[system(PostUpdate, always)] // and running while you author, too
/// ```
///
/// `always` drops the Play gate. Reach for it when the work has to
/// happen in the editor as well: a gizmo, an overlay, a streaming pump.
///
/// A stage that is not one of the fourteen is a compile error, not a
/// system that quietly stays in `Update`.
///
/// # The fourteen, in the order a frame runs them
///
/// | Stage | What belongs here |
/// |---|---|
/// | `Startup` | Once, at launch. Load, allocate, seed. |
/// | `First` | The very top of the frame. |
/// | `Input` | Reading devices into intent. |
/// | `PreUpdate` | Preparing what `Update` will need. |
/// | `Update` | **Gameplay.** Where a system goes unless it cannot. |
/// | `PostUpdate` | After gameplay — and where the engine resolves
/// |   | `Transform` into `GlobalTransform`. |
/// | `GpuSync` | Handing this frame's data to the GPU. |
/// | `Gpu` | Compute submitted with the frame's encoder. |
/// | `Physics` | Fixed timestep. **May run several times a frame, or
/// |   | none**, so never put per-frame work here. |
/// | `PostPhysics` | Same timestep, after the solver. |
/// | `PreRender` | Last chance before drawing. |
/// | `Render` | Drawing. |
/// | `PostRender` | After drawing. |
/// | `Last` | The very end of the frame. |
///
/// 🔴 **`PostUpdate` is the one that bites.** It is where a local
/// `Transform` becomes the `GlobalTransform` that meshes, lights and
/// cameras actually read. Write a transform *before* it and the change
/// lands this frame. Write it *after* — `PostUpdate`, `Gpu`, anywhere
/// later — and everything downstream renders one frame behind, forever,
/// with no error and no log line.
